.TH SCOPEBRIDGE-SERVER 1 "September 2026" "scopebridge" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
scopebridge-server \- serve a Rigol DS1000Z-E oscilloscope to GUI, terminal and script clients
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B scopebridge-server
.RB ( \-\-usb
.IR DEVICE | auto
|
.B \-\-lan
.IR HOST [: PORT ]
|
.BR \-\-sim )
.RB [ \-\-port
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-listen
.IR ADDRESS ]
.RB [ \-\-log
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-web
.I PORT
.RB [ \-\-web\-root
.IR DIR ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B scopebridge-server
owns the connection to the oscilloscope and serves it over TCP, as JSON
lines with binary payloads for waveform data, to any number of clients at
once:
.BR scopebridge-gui (1),
.BR scopebridge-term (1),
Python scripts
.RB ( scopebridge-run (1))
or any program that speaks the protocol described in
.IR PROTOCOL.md .
Besides the scope's own functions it offers what the scope cannot do
alone: spectra and zooming over the whole acquisition memory, decoding of
UART, I2C and SPI traffic from captures, timing of code that marks its
blocks on a pin, and reference waveforms shared by all clients.
.PP
Developed and tested with a Rigol DS1202Z-E (firmware 00.06.04).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-usb " DEVICE"
The scope on USB, through the kernel's usbtmc driver, e.g.
.IR /dev/usbtmc0 .
.B auto
finds the first Rigol scope (USB vendor 1ab1); the kernel numbers the
device anew each time the scope is connected.
The device is readable by root only unless a udev rule says otherwise:
.B sudo make install-udev
in the source tree installs one giving the
.B plugdev
group access
.RI ( /etc/udev/rules.d/70-rigol.rules ).
.TP
.BI \-\-lan " HOST\fR[:\fIPORT\fR]"
The scope on the network (port 5555 unless given).
.TP
.B \-\-sim
A built-in simulated scope, for trying clients without hardware.
.TP
.BI \-\-port " N"
The TCP port to serve on; default 5026.
.TP
.BI \-\-listen " ADDRESS"
The address to serve on; default 127.0.0.1. The protocol has no
authentication: only listen on other addresses on a network you trust.
.TP
.BI \-\-log " FILE"
Append a command log: a JSON line with a timestamp for every connection,
request and reply. It can be replayed with
.BR "scopebridge-run \-\-replay" .
.TP
.BI \-\-web " PORT"
Also serve the web interface on
.IR PORT ,
at the
.B \-\-listen
address: a GUI for any browser, at
.BI http:// ADDRESS : PORT /\fR,
which speaks the protocol over a WebSocket at
.BR /ws .
.TP
.BI \-\-web\-root " DIR"
Where the web interface's files are; by default
.I web/
next to the program's
.I bin/
directory (the source tree), or
.I @DATADIR@/web
(installed).
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH SERVICE
.B make install
installs a systemd user unit,
.IR scopebridge-server.service ,
which runs the server with the settings in
.IR ~/.scopebridgerc ,
and the options in
.I ~/.config/scopebridge/server.conf
if there are any
.RB ( SCOPEBRIDGE_SERVER_ARGS=... ):
.PP
.nf
.RS
printf '[server]\\nsource = lan 192.168.0.83\\n' > ~/.scopebridgerc
systemctl \-\-user enable \-\-now scopebridge-server
journalctl \-\-user \-u scopebridge-server
.RE
.fi
.PP
(with your scope's address in place of the example 192.168.0.83)
.PP
Clients then connect to it whenever they start;
.BR scopebridge (1)
uses a running server rather than starting one.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 is never returned while serving; 1 if the scope cannot be opened, the
port cannot be bound, or the arguments are wrong.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.scopebridgerc
Settings, in its
.B [server]
section:
.BR source " (" "usb auto" ", " "usb DEVICE" ", " "lan HOST[:PORT]" ", " sim ),
.BR port ,
.BR listen " and " web ,
for the options not given; without a source anywhere,
.BR "\-\-usb auto" .
.B $SCOPEBRIDGE_RC
names another file (empty: none).
.TP
.I @DOCDIR@/PROTOCOL.md
The protocol.
.TP
.I @DOCDIR@/MANUAL.md
The user manual.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR scopebridge (1),
.BR scopebridge-gui (1),
.BR scopebridge-term (1),
.BR scopebridge-run (1)
